<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description="How AI Success Network collects facts, computes data ratings, and what it does not cover."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
  <h1>How this works, and what it can’t tell you</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The short version: facts come from vendors’ own pages, ratings are computed by a fixed rule, and
    opinions are labelled as opinions.
  </p>

  <h2>Where facts come from</h2>
  <p>
    Only the vendor’s own primary sources: pricing pages, privacy policies, trust centres, help
    articles and data processing agreements. Every fact shows the short quote it rests on and the
    page it came from, with the date it was last checked. No reviews, no forums, no hearsay.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a vendor’s pages don’t clearly say something, it is recorded as <em>unclear</em>, and unclear
    counts against the tool.
  </p>

  <h2>How ratings are computed</h2>
  <p>
    A rating belongs to a <strong>plan</strong>, not a tool: the same product can be safe on one
    tier and unsuitable on another. The rule looks at five cited facts per plan.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <span class="chip red">Red</span> Anything unclear; training on your inputs with no way to
      turn it off; or the vendor’s AI subprocessors may train on your data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="chip green">Green</span> Training is off (by default or enforced by an admin), a
      data processing agreement exists, an admin can enforce settings, and AI subprocessors are
      barred from training or the vendor is itself the model provider.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="chip amber">Amber</span> Everything else. “Only after you switch off training”
      means training is on by default and you must change a personal setting your employer cannot
      enforce.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The rule is a tested function in the open-source repository. A person can override a rating, but
    only with a written reason shown beside it.
  </p>

  <h2>Facts versus opinion</h2>
  <p>
    Each tool page has two clearly separated parts. The solid-edged panel is what the vendor
    publishes plus the computed rating. The dashed panel is Cameron’s assessment — a judgement
    written by hand, never generated, and never changed by automation.
  </p>

  <h2>What this does not cover</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It is not legal advice. Check your own contract, your company’s policies, and your DPA.</li>
    <li>
      It rates data handling as the vendor describes it. It cannot tell you whether a vendor follows
      its own policy.
    </li>
    <li>Pages change. Each fact shows when it was last checked; older means less reliable.</li>
    <li>
      Prices are shown in the currency the vendor publishes, as listed on the day checked. Taxes,
      regional pricing and discounts are not included.
    </li>
    <li>
      “May need IT approval” is a flag, not a ruling: it appears when a tool joins calls with a bot
      or can only be installed on a desktop.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Spotted something wrong or out of date?
    <a href="https://github.com/Camerongmccallum24/ai-success-network/issues">Open an issue</a>
    with a link to the vendor page. Changes happen through a public pull request.
  </p>
</Base>
